WEBVTT

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:08.400
Hi, folks. My name is Eric Anderson. I'm the administrator for the town of Andover,

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.400
and today I'm going to be speaking to you about all things,

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.320
road and

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:15.200
road network.

00:00:17.595 --> 00:00:18.955
Okay. First off,

00:00:19.515 --> 00:00:23.995
roads in Andover are the single most expensive asset that the town has.

00:00:24.315 --> 00:00:27.515
We have about 34 miles of paved roads,

00:00:27.915 --> 00:00:30.890
and it would cost us about $17,000,000

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:33.530
if we had to replace that road network

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.250
and we also have about $15,000,000

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:39.530
worth of bridges and culverts in the town of Andover.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.250
One of the reasons I'm doing this presentation

00:00:43.015 --> 00:00:43.735
is

00:00:43.895 --> 00:00:46.375
I get a lot of residents

00:00:46.535 --> 00:00:49.975
expressing their frustration over the quality

00:00:49.975 --> 00:00:51.655
of the town roads.

00:00:52.215 --> 00:00:55.815
A typical conversation is hey how come I have potholes

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:00.090
or uneven pavement or this or that on my road?

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:04.010
And then the second question I get a lot is why did we fix

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:13.175
you know a particular road when we didn't fix my road, my roads worse? So that's what I'm going to be talking to you about today is the road network.

00:01:15.735 --> 00:01:16.375
So

00:01:16.695 --> 00:01:17.735
first of all,

00:01:18.375 --> 00:01:22.455
here's the things I'm going to talk about today. I'm going to talk about annual maintenance,

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.660
the public works crew,

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:26.980
the concepts and pavement management,

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:33.540
road conditions here in town, and how we evaluate the roads, and then what the plan is in Andover.

00:01:36.295 --> 00:01:41.175
So first of all the Public Works Department currently we have a three person crew

00:01:41.655 --> 00:01:45.815
although we're hoping to hire one fourth person this fall.

00:01:46.135 --> 00:01:48.375
We do have a little bit of money for a

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:50.775
summer helper that does mowing.

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:54.930
The crew also does all their own vehicle maintenance

00:01:55.010 --> 00:02:03.090
and the road crew foreman who's one of the three people is also the tree warden, the burning official, and has to inspect all the new driveway aprons.

00:02:03.675 --> 00:02:05.675
As the town administrator,

00:02:05.675 --> 00:02:08.635
I function as the public works director.

00:02:10.315 --> 00:02:14.555
So the next thing is what do we have to do every year to our roads?

00:02:14.635 --> 00:02:19.960
There's a lot of annual maintenance that we need in the roads. First, we need to sweep the roads every year.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.440
We also need to scrape the sides of the road to get out that organic

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:29.720
material that's built up and prevent the water from running off the roads properly.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.360
We need to mow the sides of the road

00:02:32.985 --> 00:02:34.825
ideally twice a year

00:02:35.065 --> 00:02:42.345
and we need to clean all the catch basins in the road. Those are those drainage structures with the grates in the road

00:02:42.665 --> 00:02:50.980
and in the bottom of the catch basin, there's something called the sump and that is where all the leaves and sand and dirt and gravel accumulate

00:02:51.140 --> 00:03:02.945
and we have to clean that out every year or else it backs up in the sump and then gets caught in the pipes where it's much harder to deal with. We also need to clean out all the waterways.

00:03:03.505 --> 00:03:04.545
We typically

00:03:04.785 --> 00:03:07.985
spend some money every year repairing guardrails

00:03:08.145 --> 00:03:11.505
and we also deal with missing and replaced

00:03:12.145 --> 00:03:12.545
signs.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:24.470
For some reason people seem to feel the need to steal our signs from time to time. We also do road striping, tree trimming, and a bunch of other things as just routine annual maintenance.

00:03:25.915 --> 00:03:30.955
Next thing I want to talk about a little bit is the difference between preservation

00:03:30.955 --> 00:03:32.555
and rehabilitation

00:03:32.635 --> 00:03:33.995
on roadways.

00:03:34.475 --> 00:03:35.755
So preservation

00:03:35.835 --> 00:03:37.115
is maintaining

00:03:37.115 --> 00:03:38.155
the good roads.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:40.720
It's relatively inexpensive.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.560
The idea is to work on the road before it gets bad

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.200
and generally keeps people happy.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.560
The second part is rehabilitation

00:03:51.075 --> 00:03:53.075
and that's what you do to fix

00:03:53.155 --> 00:03:55.635
the bad roads in the town.

00:03:56.115 --> 00:03:57.715
It's fairly expensive.

00:03:57.715 --> 00:03:59.235
It takes a lot of time.

00:03:59.555 --> 00:04:02.915
And by the time you get to that point, there's lots of cracks and potholes.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.320
And generally by the time a road gets to the point where you're rehabilitating it,

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.560
then people are unhappy.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.280
So preservation

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:14.720
is just the

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.320
job of keeping something in good condition.

00:04:19.115 --> 00:04:21.275
If you thought about it like your house,

00:04:21.755 --> 00:04:24.475
if instead of waiting for

00:04:24.715 --> 00:04:29.915
the siding on your house to completely rot away, if you had wood siding,

00:04:30.075 --> 00:04:39.160
if from time to time you scraped it and repainted it, in the long run it would be a lot cheaper than waiting till it needs to be replaced totally.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.840
And roads are the same thing. If you put money into them early,

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.040
you can save yourself from putting a lot more money into them later.

00:04:48.065 --> 00:04:51.505
So Andover's roads, some of them are pretty bad.

00:04:52.145 --> 00:04:53.185
We're

00:04:53.185 --> 00:04:54.225
not alone.

00:04:54.785 --> 00:04:59.745
Connecticut's roads in general are some of the worst in the country

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:06.130
and Andover's roads for rural roads are about as average with the rest of the state.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:07.250
Basically

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:10.210
about 14 miles of our roadway

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:11.970
are either poor or failing.

00:05:13.395 --> 00:05:16.595
So the question is why? There's three main reasons.

00:05:16.755 --> 00:05:18.995
One, we haven't been spending enough money.

00:05:19.155 --> 00:05:23.795
Two, we haven't done enough planning and three, we haven't had enough personnel.

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:30.370
So one of the things that's driving the cost of roadwork

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:34.370
is the cost of liquid asphalt cement.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:37.410
As you can see from 1990

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:40.930
to now, so over about a thirty year

00:05:40.415 --> 00:05:41.215
period,

00:05:41.375 --> 00:05:45.135
the cost of liquid asphalt has gone up about five times.

00:05:46.015 --> 00:05:50.175
And that's the major cost or one of the major costs in asphalt.

00:05:50.335 --> 00:05:53.535
So paving has gotten a lot more expensive

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:58.470
and we can't do the old days which was just pave 10% of the roads every year

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:02.150
and, you know, and keep our roads in good condition that way.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:08.630
So Andover for a long time has used a what's called the worst first approach

00:06:08.965 --> 00:06:12.165
and basically we wait till the roads get really bad,

00:06:12.645 --> 00:06:15.125
and everybody's complaining and

00:06:15.205 --> 00:06:16.645
the board of selectmen

00:06:16.645 --> 00:06:21.925
you know get sick of listening to all the complaints and they tell the public works department to repave the road.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:27.650
So that leads to high cost and it leads to poor road condition.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:32.130
In the past, the town of Andover has used crack sealing

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.690
and then paving and reconstruction

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:36.690
and then gone out and fixed potholes

00:06:36.935 --> 00:06:41.495
And that was the majority of what we've done for the last twelve to fifteen years.

00:06:42.775 --> 00:06:48.455
So now we're going to try to turn that on its head and switch to a preservation first strategy

00:06:48.615 --> 00:06:54.710
and that means we're going to be fog sealing, crack sealing, chip sealing, micro surfacing, cape sealing,

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:57.750
and considering cold in place recycling.

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:00.390
And we'll talk about all those things in a little bit.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:06.310
So this is an example that makes the case for pavement preservation.

00:07:07.015 --> 00:07:12.295
This is a road that's shared between two towns and it's divided down the center line.

00:07:12.775 --> 00:07:16.935
This whole road was completely redone at the same time,

00:07:17.415 --> 00:07:21.255
but the town that owned the road on the right side

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:22.960
chose to

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:24.640
do preservation

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:28.720
and the town on the left did not do anything

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:31.360
and you know fifteen years difference,

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.040
fifteen years later there's a huge difference in the quality of the road.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:36.480
So this

00:07:36.945 --> 00:07:40.385
picture is taken one year after the previous picture

00:07:40.625 --> 00:07:45.825
and you can see the town on the left has now filled in some of the pot holes,

00:07:45.985 --> 00:07:48.385
but the road is still really rough condition

00:07:48.705 --> 00:07:50.865
and the town on the right,

00:07:51.425 --> 00:07:51.905
they

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:56.930
they six years after the road was paved, they crack sealed it and then microsurfaced

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:58.450
it and then

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:02.290
eight years after that they crack sealed it again.

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:07.410
So half of this road is still in good shape, good to drive on and

00:08:08.155 --> 00:08:15.675
require minimum ongoing maintenance and half of this road needs to be ground up again and redone.

00:08:15.995 --> 00:08:17.435
So ultimately,

00:08:17.675 --> 00:08:19.355
the reason you do preservation

00:08:19.355 --> 00:08:22.075
is because in the long run, it saves you money

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:25.000
and you get to drive on better roads.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:34.120
So when we talk about pavement and quality of pavement, we're talking about what's known as the pavement condition index.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:43.515
This is a rating from zero to a 100 and it's a weighted average of different types of defects in the pavement like

00:08:43.595 --> 00:08:45.515
cracks and potholes

00:08:45.515 --> 00:08:46.715
and rutting

00:08:46.955 --> 00:08:48.715
and what's called raveling.

00:08:48.715 --> 00:08:54.380
And raveling is when a pavement starts to get very rough because it's losing the fine

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:55.580
aggregate

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:56.940
that's in the pavement.

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:03.500
So generally speaking when a road gets down to about 60 people are starting to complain

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:04.940
that

00:09:05.285 --> 00:09:10.805
you know they don't like the road and that it needs to be to be something needs to be done there.

00:09:12.405 --> 00:09:14.965
So this graph represents

00:09:14.965 --> 00:09:16.645
the deterioration

00:09:16.645 --> 00:09:18.165
curve of asphalt.

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:22.200
In year zero, you've just repaved

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:26.760
and you've made a beautiful new road that's in perfect condition.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:30.920
And then for about ten years, the road seems really nice

00:09:31.155 --> 00:09:40.115
and then very quickly over the next five years it goes from really nice to really bad. This is what happens if you don't do anything to the road.

00:09:40.515 --> 00:09:42.755
So pavement preservation

00:09:43.075 --> 00:09:46.675
is the idea that you're going to apply treatments

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:47.440
to the road

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.160
to shift the curve

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:54.960
to the right so you can go longer before you have to,

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:59.920
you know do what's called an overlay or add pavement to the road.

00:10:00.445 --> 00:10:04.045
So this also lists out the various different techniques

00:10:04.285 --> 00:10:08.045
and at what road quality or road PCI

00:10:08.045 --> 00:10:09.325
are they best applied.

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:17.020
So this is another example of the case for preserving

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:19.340
roads versus rehabilitating

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:20.140
roads.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:24.060
The curve in blue is a strategy of preservation

00:10:24.615 --> 00:10:34.615
and you can see the overall quality of the road never gets down to the point where people are complaining about it because it only gets down to a PCI of about 60 and

00:10:35.255 --> 00:10:45.410
the green is what happens if you only pave. You basically wait fifteen years till the road is really bad and everybody's complaining, then you pave it, then you wait fifteen years,

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:47.890
you know, and you keep cycling through that.

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:48.770
So

00:10:49.090 --> 00:10:49.650
preservation

00:10:50.205 --> 00:10:54.845
is a lower cost and maintains a better overall road quality

00:10:55.005 --> 00:10:57.805
and leads to generally happier people.

00:11:00.205 --> 00:11:03.805
So what types of things are pavement preservation?

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:05.400
In the

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:10.280
upper left is an example of crack sealing.

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:12.360
This is a crew using,

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:14.600
something called PCRM,

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:19.560
which is a compound specifically for filling cracks and roadways

00:11:19.715 --> 00:11:23.795
and keeping water from getting under the road and causing problems.

00:11:24.355 --> 00:11:25.715
Then the next

00:11:25.955 --> 00:11:32.675
picture on the top is an example of a micro surfacing material being applied to the road

00:11:33.075 --> 00:11:34.355
and then on the

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:36.650
bottom left

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:41.290
is a fog seal being applied over a chip sealed road

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:47.450
and then the one all the way to the bottom right shows a very thin overlay

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:50.330
of asphalt being applied to a road.

00:11:53.945 --> 00:11:56.825
Okay so next I'd like to talk about

00:11:57.145 --> 00:12:03.545
how we think about roads aging. So the town of Andover has 34 miles of year miles of road

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:08.290
and every year all those roads age and get one year older.

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:14.370
So you can think of that as 34 mile years of aging of our road network per year

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:22.865
and if we maintain roads and we add 34 mile years of maintenance to the road, our roads stay the same.

00:12:23.345 --> 00:12:27.825
If we do more, the roads get better. If we do less, the roads get worse.

00:12:29.745 --> 00:12:33.850
Each treatment or each maintenance thing that we do to the road,

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:43.530
we think about the number of miles of treatment that we're doing times how much life it adds to the road and we get mile years restored

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:48.355
to the road, which is also called the added network service life.

00:12:50.035 --> 00:12:57.075
And again as long as we're adding more to the roads than the roads are aging, the overall road network is getting better.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:02.180
So we also look at something called the equivalent annual cost

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:08.340
and basically what that is is the cost of a treatment times its effectiveness

00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:12.740
and on the left we list out all the different treatment alternatives

00:13:13.755 --> 00:13:15.515
that we do in town

00:13:15.675 --> 00:13:16.475
and

00:13:16.555 --> 00:13:19.515
then on the extreme right

00:13:19.675 --> 00:13:21.275
is the cost

00:13:21.595 --> 00:13:26.315
per square foot per year and you can see you get

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:31.440
much more bang for your buck from crack sealing than you do reconstruction.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:35.920
In fact, if you let roads get all the way to the point where you need to reconstruct,

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:38.080
that's about five times as expensive

00:13:38.475 --> 00:13:42.555
as if you surface treated them early in their life expectancy.

00:13:44.315 --> 00:13:45.915
So this is the same

00:13:46.235 --> 00:13:48.875
slide but in graphic terms,

00:13:49.035 --> 00:13:59.730
you can see that it's much more expensive to do crack sealing, fog sealing, chip sealing than it is to wait until you need to mill and fill or rehabilitate your paper.

00:14:01.090 --> 00:14:05.890
So what is a fog seal? A fog seal is a light application

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:07.330
of an emulsion

00:14:07.725 --> 00:14:10.525
and an emulsion is basically just

00:14:10.685 --> 00:14:15.645
asphalt plus a rejuvenator plus a water all mixed up together

00:14:16.765 --> 00:14:19.645
into a fine milky looking substance

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:24.060
and it gets applied to newer pavement as a protectant

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:31.260
and it's applied in roads that look really good, don't have any cracks in them,

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.500
that look like new pavement.

00:14:34.865 --> 00:14:37.985
That's the perfect time to be applying a fog seal.

00:14:38.385 --> 00:14:41.185
So they protect the road against oxidation,

00:14:41.185 --> 00:14:51.800
they protect against moisture getting down into the road and they protect against raveling which is the fine material, the sand in a road from getting

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:52.520
lost.

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:54.120
They're low cost,

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:55.400
highly effective,

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:59.400
and they delay the need for more expensive repairs later.

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:02.760
Basically what they do is they replace a series of compounds

00:15:03.185 --> 00:15:05.745
that are in the asphalt called maltines

00:15:05.905 --> 00:15:09.825
that get lost just from sunlight and weather in general.

00:15:11.585 --> 00:15:14.225
So the next thing we do is crack

00:15:14.465 --> 00:15:18.225
and crack seal is a modified asphalt

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:19.730
that is put on the road

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:22.530
very hot and then it

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:24.610
cools and hardens

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:26.450
but it stays flexible.

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:32.850
So what its job is to seal the road and keep moisture from getting down into the road and damaging the road.

00:15:34.565 --> 00:15:42.245
And the guy in the green shirt is putting a material over the top of it, applying it by hand that keeps you from,

00:15:42.485 --> 00:15:47.045
your tires from sticking to the crack seal if you drive over right after it's sealed.

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:53.550
So crack seal is generally applied to roads starting

00:15:53.870 --> 00:16:05.485
about five years after the road is paved when the cracks first start to appear and you're going to apply that you know in the five to seven year range of a road.

00:16:07.405 --> 00:16:11.805
And what it does is it reduces potholes and sub base failures

00:16:11.805 --> 00:16:18.380
because it keeps water from getting down into there and causing freeze thaw damage in the winter.

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:24.380
It also minimizes impact on traffic in the neighborhoods and it's again very cost effective.

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:29.020
So the next thing we want to talk about is a chip seal,

00:16:29.605 --> 00:16:34.885
And so a chip seal can be used on pavement that's in fair to good condition

00:16:35.125 --> 00:16:37.125
that has moderate cracking.

00:16:37.685 --> 00:16:40.005
And if you have big irregularities

00:16:40.005 --> 00:16:43.125
in the road surface like big dips and stuff like that,

00:16:43.285 --> 00:16:44.805
you should do what's called shimming,

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:51.070
which is putting a leveling course of asphalt over a portion of the road before you do it.

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:56.350
And a chip seal can be combined with another technique called microsurfacing

00:16:57.565 --> 00:16:59.085
for basically

00:16:59.085 --> 00:17:00.525
a double layer

00:17:00.685 --> 00:17:01.405
seal.

00:17:01.885 --> 00:17:02.445
So

00:17:02.925 --> 00:17:07.965
many of you are familiar with this, we have just done this on the number of roads in the town of Andover.

00:17:10.460 --> 00:17:13.260
So the next thing is microsurfacing,

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:15.340
and microsurfacing

00:17:15.340 --> 00:17:16.860
is a single

00:17:17.580 --> 00:17:20.060
or double layer of material

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:23.405
that's put down its asphalt emulsion,

00:17:23.725 --> 00:17:25.645
a very fine stone

00:17:25.645 --> 00:17:27.565
plus a mineral fiber

00:17:27.805 --> 00:17:32.765
and it's applied as a surface treatment on the road to bring back skid resistance

00:17:34.060 --> 00:17:37.580
and also smooth out the road. So it is typically

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:43.100
applied to roads that are in about you know an 80 to 75

00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:45.420
PCI condition

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:48.060
and if it's used in conjunction

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:58.765
with chip seal it can be used on roads that are worse than that. So you chip seal first and then micro seal over and that's commonly called a cape seal.

00:18:00.765 --> 00:18:04.445
So this is what it looks like when they're micro surfacing

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:08.980
and micro surfacing is a way to prevent the pavement underneath

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:11.620
from oxidation and moisture intrusion

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:14.660
and it restores the good surface characteristics

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:19.860
of the road but doesn't really raise it up so much that you have to adjust your catch basins

00:18:20.585 --> 00:18:23.145
or worry about changes in the runoff

00:18:23.945 --> 00:18:30.665
you know into people's driveways and stuff like that. It also provides a beneficial increase in skid resistance.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:37.400
So a cape seal as we just talked about is a combination of the two things.

00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:41.320
It's first a chip seal that's applied and then the

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:46.040
micro seal is applied or micro surfacing is applied on top of that.

00:18:48.605 --> 00:18:50.125
Okay. And again,

00:18:50.285 --> 00:18:52.045
a cape seal road,

00:18:52.765 --> 00:18:56.125
you know, the road has to be structurally in good shape,

00:18:56.365 --> 00:19:00.925
but the pavement can be, you know, quite a bit farther down the spectrum,

00:19:01.565 --> 00:19:02.765
and still be effective.

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:11.660
So again cape seal first step chip seal, second step micro seal over the top of that.

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:13.420
And we're

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:16.140
going to try to use cape seals

00:19:16.495 --> 00:19:22.495
whenever we're on a road that we know has really high pedestrian traffic such as the Lake District.

00:19:25.135 --> 00:19:28.975
So chip seal plus fog seal is the other alternative.

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:34.010
Many of the roads like Juravadi or Wales Road, we have chip sealed

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:38.330
and then we're planning on going over the top of the chip seal

00:19:38.410 --> 00:19:40.570
with another fog seal.

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:46.135
And what that does is that fog seal kind of locks in the chips,

00:19:46.295 --> 00:19:52.375
provides good retention and it also smooths out the road. So you end up with a road that

00:19:52.535 --> 00:19:55.895
is a lot better, can take a lot more snowplow damage

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:56.960
and

00:19:57.040 --> 00:20:00.000
is a little smoother than a straight chip seal road.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:02.640
So

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:06.000
now we're going to turn away from

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:07.440
techniques

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:10.185
and talk about how we determine

00:20:10.185 --> 00:20:12.985
what the condition of our roadwork is.

00:20:14.025 --> 00:20:16.825
When I first took over as the administrator,

00:20:16.825 --> 00:20:21.465
one of the things I did was I went around and I used a program

00:20:21.545 --> 00:20:23.945
that was freeware from the state of Maine

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:27.700
that our town engineer had previously

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:28.820
evaluated

00:20:28.820 --> 00:20:32.820
the roads and I repeated that so we had data from two years apart.

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:43.195
But I also realized that we needed a better method and that just relying on me to evaluate the roads wasn't the best plan.

00:20:43.595 --> 00:20:45.995
So we looked at a number of different

00:20:46.235 --> 00:20:49.355
companies, engineering firms that provide that service

00:20:49.675 --> 00:20:52.315
and we settled on a company called StreetScan

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:54.610
which does an automated

00:20:54.610 --> 00:20:56.210
roadway evaluation

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:57.010
program.

00:20:57.250 --> 00:21:00.850
Basically they drive over the roads and they have a really

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:03.810
efficient camera system

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:09.855
that takes pictures of the road every 10 feet and then an automated program

00:21:10.335 --> 00:21:11.615
compares

00:21:11.615 --> 00:21:12.655
that with

00:21:13.055 --> 00:21:23.700
you know what the quality of the road is. And then they go back manually and they look at 10% of the segments to make sure that the computer did a good job of

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:25.780
evaluating

00:21:25.780 --> 00:21:27.300
the quality of the road.

00:21:28.020 --> 00:21:31.860
So one of the advantages of that is it takes out the human element

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:33.860
and it gives you a completely

00:21:34.625 --> 00:21:37.905
you know, biased look at your road network.

00:21:39.025 --> 00:21:43.025
So when you're done scanning the roads, you get something back,

00:21:43.505 --> 00:21:46.065
in a program called StreetLogic,

00:21:46.065 --> 00:21:58.870
which is a really interesting program to deal with And it gives you what the average quality of your road and then it gives you the PCI of each road segment that you have in the town.

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:08.725
And it also tells you what your total backlog of maintenance and in our case, it tells us that we have about $4,800,000

00:22:08.965 --> 00:22:11.765
of roadwork backlog

00:22:11.845 --> 00:22:14.325
to get our roads up to a perfect condition.

00:22:15.845 --> 00:22:17.605
So it's an online program,

00:22:17.990 --> 00:22:26.070
It's fully customizable and has a budget and repair evaluation modules built in. So it lets you do a lot of

00:22:26.870 --> 00:22:27.990
analysis

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:31.830
of your roadwork and optimize how you're going to go about repairing.

00:22:33.795 --> 00:22:38.755
So the average PCI in Andover is 62 and

00:22:38.755 --> 00:22:42.275
for an ideal road network it would be 80

00:22:42.515 --> 00:22:46.195
to 90 depending on how picky you are about your roads.

00:22:46.595 --> 00:22:48.115
Again, Andover

00:22:49.160 --> 00:22:53.480
is quite a bit behind in paving and pavement preservation

00:22:53.480 --> 00:22:54.360
funding.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:59.800
Our average road are about three quarters of the way through their useful lifespan.

00:23:00.405 --> 00:23:02.405
So think about it another way,

00:23:02.885 --> 00:23:07.765
in a perfect world, if your roads were halfway through their useful lifespan,

00:23:07.925 --> 00:23:10.965
that means you were putting in the correct amount of maintenance

00:23:11.285 --> 00:23:12.885
and about 42%

00:23:12.885 --> 00:23:16.245
of our roads are either poor, very poor, or failing.

00:23:17.580 --> 00:23:20.780
The other thing to keep in mind is when a road gets below

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:23.340
a PCI of about 55,

00:23:23.580 --> 00:23:24.860
it deteriorates

00:23:24.860 --> 00:23:25.820
very fast.

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:28.380
So

00:23:28.780 --> 00:23:36.935
the next question is what's our plan for dealing with it? First of all, we're assuming that we're going to get money from the DOT,

00:23:37.495 --> 00:23:39.415
what's called Town Aid Road,

00:23:40.215 --> 00:23:41.575
and that this

00:23:41.655 --> 00:23:46.375
budget season we're in now, we're going to be able to budget about $300,000

00:23:46.375 --> 00:23:47.095
for roads

00:23:47.490 --> 00:23:50.050
and we're going to get about a $100,000

00:23:50.050 --> 00:23:52.290
to spend from Town Aid Road

00:23:53.090 --> 00:23:56.530
and we'll spend the $75,000

00:23:56.530 --> 00:23:58.290
from the nineteen-twenty

00:23:58.290 --> 00:23:59.090
budget

00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:01.410
this paving season also.

00:24:02.985 --> 00:24:05.625
So the plan is to use

00:24:06.425 --> 00:24:12.025
street scans planning tools and we're going to prioritize based on cost effectiveness,

00:24:12.425 --> 00:24:14.905
prioritize roads with more traffic,

00:24:16.400 --> 00:24:17.520
preservation

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:19.600
over reconstruction,

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:22.000
and long term improvements

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:24.800
over, you know, short term improvements.

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:36.205
So we're also going to try a bunch of new techniques and we're going to evaluate their performance to make sure they're doing what we think they're going to do and that they're cost effective here in the town of Andover.

00:24:36.765 --> 00:24:38.845
And we've shifted from

00:24:39.165 --> 00:24:41.885
spending most of our money on rehabilitation

00:24:41.885 --> 00:24:45.510
to now spending about half of our money on preservation

00:24:45.670 --> 00:24:48.550
and half of our money on rehabilitation.

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:55.430
So first goal is to preserve the good to moderate roads

00:24:55.430 --> 00:24:58.150
and to prevent further deterioration

00:24:58.715 --> 00:25:01.755
simply because it's much more cost effective.

00:25:03.595 --> 00:25:06.715
Goal number two, make sure that what we're doing for

00:25:07.035 --> 00:25:08.635
roadwork presentation

00:25:09.195 --> 00:25:23.520
aligns with the drainage work we have to do in town cause it's not cost effective to go treat your road and then immediately turn around the next year and tear it up because you've got to change out a bunch of culverts and a bunch of drainage pipes.

00:25:25.615 --> 00:25:34.895
So the plan. So we're going to use a series of different techniques. We're going to use crack seal and we're subbing that out to a company called Indus.

00:25:35.375 --> 00:25:36.255
We've

00:25:36.255 --> 00:25:38.415
had really good luck with them in the past

00:25:38.590 --> 00:25:40.590
and we're using a

00:25:40.910 --> 00:25:42.590
crop bid for that.

00:25:43.070 --> 00:25:48.670
We're going to use fog seals, two different types, actually three different types.

00:25:49.070 --> 00:25:50.830
We're going to use a rejuvenating

00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:51.710
fog seal.

00:25:52.375 --> 00:25:56.695
We're going to use a separate different one on our walking track,

00:25:57.015 --> 00:25:59.495
and then we're also going to use a straight

00:26:00.455 --> 00:26:04.695
non rejuvenating fog seal over our chip sealed roads.

00:26:05.240 --> 00:26:10.120
We're going to put leveling shims of pavement on some of our roads.

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:12.280
We're going to chip seal.

00:26:12.440 --> 00:26:17.400
We're going to fog seal. We're going to micro seal over the chip seal.

00:26:17.985 --> 00:26:23.585
And we're also going to do some paving and we're going to spend some time doing pothole repair.

00:26:25.985 --> 00:26:29.585
So crack sealing, we're going to spend about $50,000

00:26:29.585 --> 00:26:33.185
and we're prioritizing those roads that we're going to be chip sealing

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:36.910
and surface treating in the next budget cycle.

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:39.790
So

00:26:40.910 --> 00:26:41.950
fog seal,

00:26:42.030 --> 00:26:43.870
rejuvenating fog seal,

00:26:43.950 --> 00:26:47.310
we're going to do Windrush, E Street, Townsend and Sunset

00:26:47.475 --> 00:26:52.115
and then we're going to switch to a different compound for the walking track for two reasons.

00:26:52.355 --> 00:26:54.355
One, it's better for the grass

00:26:54.515 --> 00:26:57.555
and the second reason is that

00:26:57.875 --> 00:26:58.835
the

00:26:59.715 --> 00:27:02.835
more environmentally friendly one can be applied cold,

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:09.820
which means we don't need a large truck so we'll do less damage to the field while we're doing it.

00:27:10.060 --> 00:27:14.940
And we're also going to leave Ryan Road which was paved at the same time as Townsend.

00:27:15.100 --> 00:27:25.915
We're not going to fog seal it and that way we can watch those roads for a couple years and see how effective the fog ceiling has been in you know slowing down deterioration.

00:27:28.155 --> 00:27:29.515
So shimming,

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:34.150
So one of the things we're going to do is we're going to shim the roads

00:27:34.390 --> 00:27:39.110
prior to chip sealing. So we're going to shim as part of next year's

00:27:39.350 --> 00:27:40.150
paving

00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:42.710
or next year's chip sealing plan.

00:27:43.295 --> 00:27:46.735
We're also going to do the bridge on Merritt Valley Road.

00:27:47.055 --> 00:27:57.215
We're going to shim the sides of the bridge and the reason is right now we didn't pave all the way to the sides of the bridge and water is going off the sides of the bridge

00:27:57.800 --> 00:28:00.280
going down into the stone foundation

00:28:00.280 --> 00:28:05.240
of the bridge and causing deterioration and we need to stop that so we don't,

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:09.240
have to rebuild that bridge anytime soon.

00:28:09.560 --> 00:28:15.685
We're also planning on doing Shawty Mill Road after we finish the drainage work, which will be the next project up

00:28:15.765 --> 00:28:16.565
after

00:28:16.565 --> 00:28:18.565
we finish the transfer station

00:28:18.805 --> 00:28:24.005
and we're going to shim the transfer station where we've torn up the asphalt rebuilding

00:28:24.165 --> 00:28:24.965
the walls.

00:28:26.080 --> 00:28:32.400
And if we're done with that and we still have money, we have a bunch of other streets we'd like to shim.

00:28:34.480 --> 00:28:41.175
So chip seal. We're going to spend a fair amount of money chip sealing this year. We're doing Jurabadi,

00:28:41.175 --> 00:28:44.935
School, Lakeside, Riverside, Oak Farm, Stanley, Chesterbrook,

00:28:44.935 --> 00:28:45.895
and Wales.

00:28:46.375 --> 00:28:54.135
If we're lucky at the end of the paving season we'll be able to sneak in a chip seal repair of the transfer station also.

00:28:56.190 --> 00:28:58.190
So now we turn from,

00:28:58.750 --> 00:28:59.870
the preservation

00:28:59.870 --> 00:29:01.550
side to the reconstruction

00:29:01.550 --> 00:29:02.190
side.

00:29:02.590 --> 00:29:04.270
Shimmings also reconstruction

00:29:04.270 --> 00:29:06.830
but but it can be considered either.

00:29:07.070 --> 00:29:10.350
So we have about a $140,000

00:29:10.065 --> 00:29:11.585
to repave

00:29:11.825 --> 00:29:17.105
and we're looking at a couple different options either mill and then pave or just pave

00:29:17.505 --> 00:29:26.1000
and we're looking at Shawty Mill Road after we're done with the drainage work and also Long Hill Road from Route 6 to just past Bear Swamp.

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:30.520
We know we need to repave over the bridge deck

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:32.200
on Long Hill Road

00:29:32.600 --> 00:29:35.640
and we're also going to be tearing up some of the asphalt

00:29:35.915 --> 00:29:42.955
when we do the connectivity grant project. So afterward it would be good to just pave it and get that road done.

00:29:44.395 --> 00:29:50.875
So the last thing I wanted to talk about and one of the things people have the most issues with are potholes

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:53.590
and pavement patching.

00:29:53.990 --> 00:29:57.350
So traditionally we do one of two things. We either

00:29:57.670 --> 00:29:59.750
cut out the bad section,

00:30:00.710 --> 00:30:03.830
we saw cut it out, remove the old asphalt,

00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:09.645
and then we hand fill it with hot asphalt from an asphalt plant

00:30:09.805 --> 00:30:12.205
and then use a vibrator

00:30:12.285 --> 00:30:15.805
and pack it down and that makes a good patch.

00:30:16.845 --> 00:30:19.965
But the problem is it's very time consuming

00:30:20.540 --> 00:30:25.340
and we also waste a lot of material because we don't have any way to keep the pavement

00:30:25.420 --> 00:30:42.935
hot when we get it. So when we get the new asphalt from the plant, it comes really hot and the whole time it's being driven here and put in the ground, it's cooling off as we don't have any we don't have what's called a hot bath, a hot box which would keep it hot. So we lose a lot of material

00:30:43.255 --> 00:30:45.895
and it's a kind of inefficient process.

00:30:46.295 --> 00:30:48.775
We also use something called cold patch

00:30:49.300 --> 00:31:01.860
that can be done at any time of the year. It's a material that kind of chemically cures when you take it out of the bag and you basically put it in the hole and you pound it down with a hand tamper.

00:31:02.755 --> 00:31:07.315
The problem is it doesn't last very long and it's really expensive.

00:31:07.715 --> 00:31:13.715
The good part is you can do it when it's really cold so you can winter patch with that material.

00:31:15.280 --> 00:31:17.840
So what are we going to do differently this year?

00:31:18.160 --> 00:31:20.080
Last year we tried a

00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:22.800
technique called mastic sealing

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:25.520
using Indus as a contractor

00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:28.080
and

00:31:28.135 --> 00:31:34.535
proven to be a durable and long lasting patch but it's really expensive. We just looked at the numbers

00:31:35.015 --> 00:31:40.695
and decided we you know we couldn't do enough of it to do us any good.

00:31:41.480 --> 00:31:44.520
So this year we're going to try a different piece of equipment,

00:31:44.840 --> 00:31:46.680
something called the dura patcher

00:31:46.760 --> 00:31:50.040
and basically it has a big liquid,

00:31:50.600 --> 00:31:52.440
you know, liquid tank

00:31:52.760 --> 00:32:02.035
and then you also have stone on the truck and it can mix those two as you go and put that material into the patch.

00:32:03.235 --> 00:32:05.635
So this is what it looks like in action.

00:32:05.875 --> 00:32:09.075
There's our regular truck. This is the part we rent

00:32:09.460 --> 00:32:10.180
and,

00:32:10.660 --> 00:32:13.060
basically first you blow out the crack,

00:32:13.620 --> 00:32:15.620
then you spray in

00:32:15.700 --> 00:32:20.340
first the hot liquid which acts as kind of a tack coat so it binds to it,

00:32:20.580 --> 00:32:26.425
and then you spray in a mix of stone and hot asphalt which is basically what paving is anyway,

00:32:26.745 --> 00:32:36.665
and then you spray some loose stone on top. So the great thing about it is it's really fast and it seems to produce a long lasting patch which are the two things we need.

00:32:38.750 --> 00:32:44.590
So overall, here's what we're looking at spending. Crack sealing about $50,000

00:32:44.910 --> 00:32:48.270
chip sealing about $200,000

00:32:48.430 --> 00:32:55.645
and some of that will be the cost of the micro seal that goes over the top of the chip seal.

00:32:56.365 --> 00:32:59.565
We're also going to be spending about $75,000

00:32:59.565 --> 00:33:01.725
or so shimming roads,

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:04.400
about $140,000

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:06.080
on paving of roads,

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:08.160
and about $20,000

00:33:08.160 --> 00:33:09.920
on pothole repairs.

00:33:10.480 --> 00:33:15.055
And that's of course subject to money and

00:33:15.055 --> 00:33:19.695
the getting, you know, effective bids back from contractors.

00:33:21.855 --> 00:33:23.695
So that's it.

00:33:24.655 --> 00:33:25.295
And,

00:33:25.695 --> 00:33:27.135
if you have any questions,

00:33:27.730 --> 00:33:32.290
you can always email me my email is e anderson

00:33:33.064 --> 00:33:34.264
andoverct

00:33:34.264 --> 00:33:37.384
dot org and I thank you for

00:33:37.624 --> 00:33:39.464
taking the time to watch this

00:33:39.784 --> 00:33:40.664
and,

00:33:41.464 --> 00:33:42.344
that's it.

00:33:42.664 --> 00:33:43.624
Have a wonderful day.
